The library sends a plain-text list of donations. The first two
lines are a heading and a date, and the remaining lines name the
boxes that arrived. You want to count the boxes without counting
the heading. Rather than opening an editor and deleting the first
two lines, you can ask one command to skip them and another to
count what remains. You don't need another file just to pass a
short list between the two.

\begin{objectives}
\item Read a pipeline as a series of connections between commands.
\item Test stages independently, and use \texttt{tee} to retain an intermediate result.
\item Find failures that the last stage's status can hide.
\end{objectives}

\section{Remove a heading without editing a file}
\label{sec:17-first}
Start a practice \cmd|sh| shell if necessary; return to your
usual shell with \cmd|exit| at the end. The first input line
makes a new private directory, saves its path as \texttt{lab},
and enters it before printing the path. Stop if it fails and
record the path if it succeeds. In the new directory only,
\cmd|printf| writes the fixture with a newline after each phrase.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" && pwd
printf '%s\n' 'Donation list' 'Saturday' 'Mystery box' \
  'Poetry box' 'Garden box' > donations.txt
\end{terminal}

The backslash at the end of the second input line immediately
precedes the newline. It continues the same command onto the
next line; do not put a space after that backslash. The indentation
on the continuation line is only for readability. The shell's
\cmd|>| opens a fresh output file here and would truncate it if
it already existed.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 donations.txt
\end{terminal}

\cmd|tail -n +3| prints from line three through the end,
counting the first line as one. The plus sign matters: plain
\cmd|tail -n 3| instead takes the last three lines. The answers
coincide in this small fixture but would differ if another box
arrived. Think of \cmd|+3| as an address at which to start and
\cmd|3| as the size of the ending piece. The output here is the
three lines naming boxes. \cmd|tail| sends the result to standard
output without modifying the original file.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 donations.txt | wc -l
\end{terminal}

Read \cmd+|+ as ``connect standard output on the left to standard
input on the right.'' This connection is called a \emph{pipe};
the complete arrangement is a \emph{pipeline}. The shell starts
both stages and provides the connections. The second stage can
start reading as the first supplies bytes; it need not wait for a
completed file. The count itself waits until the incoming stream
ends. Expect three without a filename because \cmd|wc -l| receives
its data through standard input and has no file operand. It
counts newline characters; here each box occupies one completed
line.
\index{pipeline}%
\index{pipe}%
\index{tail@\texttt{tail}}%

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-17/donations.tikz}
\caption{The counter reads the tail's output, not the original file.}
\label{fig:17-donations}
\VisualDescription{The donations file goes into tail; only the box lines travel through the pipe from tail to wc. The count goes from wc to the terminal.}
\end{figure}

Try to say what crosses the connection without using the names of
the commands. Here it's ``three lines, one per box, in the
original order.'' That is enough to predict the count. For a
larger job, the same sentence can uncover a mismatched assumption:
perhaps the first command writes a decorative heading, or maybe
one logical record spans multiple lines. The pipe itself doesn't
know what a heading, record, or line means. It transports bytes.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.5in}Y}
\toprule
Part of this request & What it knows \\
\midrule
tail -n +3 & Starts at a line number; doesn't recognize donation boxes. \\
Pipe & Carries bytes in order; doesn't label or store a report for later. \\
wc -l & Counts newlines; doesn't know what was skipped. \\
Shell & Connects the commands; doesn't decide whether three is a meaningful total. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Check the middle, not just the end}
\label{sec:17-inspect}
A count of three could describe three valid boxes, three
headings, or three blank lines. Start by running the left stage
alone with the small fixture. When you want both a record of
its exact output and a downstream count, insert \cmd|tee|.
It copies standard input to standard output and to each file
operand. With no append option it replaces a previous file of
that name, so use the new practice name shown here.
\index{tee@\texttt{tee}}%

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 donations.txt | tee box-rows.txt | wc -l
cat box-rows.txt
\end{terminal}

The number is still three. The saved lines identify the boxes;
the number itself is not in \texttt{box-rows.txt}. Inspect
the position of \cmd|tee| before deciding whether it saves the
part you care about. The following captures the other side of
the count as a separate experiment:

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 donations.txt | wc -l | tee total.txt
cat total.txt
\end{terminal}

Now you get the count once from \cmd|tee| and again when you
read \texttt{total.txt}. See Figure~\ref{fig:17-tee} for the
first arrangement. The important choice is not whether to keep
a file but which stage's output to keep. Saving intermediates
can make a muddled pipeline easier to debug. Once the stages
are understood, you may decide the intermediate copy isn't
useful. Don't store a copy of sensitive data accidentally while
testing a real job.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-17/tee.tikz}
\caption{\texttt{tee} forwards a stream and also writes a copy.}
\label{fig:17-tee}
\VisualDescription{Box lines flow from tail into tee. One branch sends a copy to box-rows.txt and the other passes the same lines to the counter.}
\end{figure}

For a beginner's check, it is also reasonable to use two
separate commands with an explicitly named intermediate file.
This takes disk space and creates cleanup work but it gives
you a chance to check the first command before calling the
second. Here \cmd|&&| asks the shell to run the count only
if \cmd|tail| reports success; it does not judge the quality
of the result.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 donations.txt > checked-rows.txt &&
wc -l < checked-rows.txt
\end{terminal}

If the first command fails, inspect the diagnostic and the
possibly empty or partial intermediate file. If it works,
verify that the rows make sense before trusting the count.
The small expense of retaining intermediate data can be
worth paying when a count is going into a report that others
will rely on.

\section{A successful count can follow a failed read}
\label{sec:17-status}
\index{pipeline status}%
The normal pipe connects standard output, not standard error.
In this example the input path doesn't exist. \cmd|tail| has no
rows to send, and its diagnostic normally remains visible on the
terminal. \cmd|wc| can still count the empty stream correctly.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 not-here.txt | wc -l
printf 'Pipeline status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Expect a missing-file diagnostic, a count of zero, and status
zero in ordinary \cmd|sh| with \texttt{pipefail} not enabled.
The two messages are independent; exact wording and their
relative order can vary. The zero count describes the input
received by the counter, not a real inventory of a missing
file. The zero status normally reports the final command's
success, not the success of all stages. This is a place where
printing an extra result can obscure the actual problem
rather than clarify it.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-17/failure.tikz}
\caption{The last stage can succeed using an empty stream.}
\label{fig:17-failure}
\VisualDescription{The missing file makes tail report a diagnostic outside the pipe. The pipe contains no rows; the counter counts zero and returns success.}
\end{figure}

One way to diagnose is to run individual stages. This example
uses exactly the left command from the failed pipeline, with
no pipe, followed immediately by the status check.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 not-here.txt
printf 'Tail status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Now the status is nonzero. A useful habit when assembling a
pipeline is to run each prefix against a known small input:
first the producer alone; then producer and next stage;
finally add the last stage. Don't keep adding commands to make
an unexpected result less visible. Also watch for a stage that
takes a file operand and therefore doesn't read the piped
data. It might be giving you a valid answer about the wrong file.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.55in}Y}
\toprule
Observation & What to investigate \\
\midrule
Count zero, no diagnostic & Was a genuinely empty stream expected? Inspect the producer's output. \\
Count zero, missing-file message & The producer failed. Fix or verify the input, not the counter. \\
Count looks plausible & Check one intermediate result; sensible-looking output can describe the wrong rows. \\
Pipeline status nonzero & Read the command-specific diagnostics. Split the pipeline to identify the stage. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{platformnote}{POSIX sh, Bash, and Zsh}
POSIX.1-2024 specifies a \texttt{pipefail} option, but older
POSIX editions did not and deployed shells vary. Bash and
Zsh provide \cmd|set -o pipefail|; do not assume it is present
in the \cmd|sh| you started. With it enabled, the pipeline
can report failure by a stage even when its last command succeeds.
Check the local shell manual or use separately checked stages.
In Bash, run \cmd|bash| to start a disposable child shell
if available, then try:
\begin{terminal}
set -o pipefail
tail -n +3 not-here.txt | wc -l
printf 'Pipeline status: %s\n' "$?"
exit
\end{terminal}
The count remains zero but the status is nonzero. Leaving the
child restores your previous shell with its own options unchanged.
\end{platformnote}

Don't interpret that option as ``the data is correct if the
status is zero.'' It checks command completion, not your choice
of stages. Some intentional pipelines use a reader that
finishes early, and a producer that tries to write more after
the reader has gone can fail. Chapter~\ref{ch:31} introduces
signals and command termination; until then, use a small,
complete stream when learning what a multi-stage status means.
Also resist piping a diagnostic into a data counter just to
hide it: \cmd|2>&1| can make the missing-file message count
as a box. Keep errors separate when rows need to remain data.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Three or none?}
\Brian{When the input file was missing, the pipeline printed zero. Is that a bug?}
\Stephen{The counter received no lines, so zero is the right count for its input.}
\Brian{But I asked how many boxes were in the donation list.}
\Stephen{The first stage couldn't read the list. The counter didn't know why there were no lines.}
\Brian{I checked the status, and that was zero too.}
\Stephen{Ordinarily that's the last stage's answer. Test the producer separately, or use an available pipeline failure option.}
\Brian{Then I can report zero only after checking where the rows came from.}
\Stephen{Right. Don't turn ``no data arrived'' into ``the list was empty.''}
\DialogueConclusion{A valid count can describe incomplete input.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Make each stage answer a smaller question}
\label{sec:17-contract}
Suppose the organizer adds a fourth box, but also leaves
a blank line in the list. Is the answer now four or five?
The existing pipeline counts every newline after the
heading, so its answer depends on the shape of the input,
not on whether a line actually describes a box. It was
correct for the original fixture because each box occupied
exactly one line and there were no blank lines. If that
guarantee is no longer true, inspect the output from the
first stage before trying to fix the count. The ability
to insert a filter is useful only once you know what the
filter should keep. Chapter~\ref{ch:21} introduces filters
that can work with more explicit row and field rules.

There is another easy mistake: ask the second stage to
read the original file by name even though you've piped
the selected rows into it.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 donations.txt | wc -l donations.txt
\end{terminal}

Expect five and the filename, not three. The right stage
has a file operand so it reads that file rather than
counting the pipe. With this tiny fixture the left stage's
ordinary data either fits in the pipe or the command can
encounter a closed reader, depending on timing. In neither
case are the rows its successor counts. The extra pathname
at the end hasn't made the original pipeline safer; it has
made it answer a different question. Trace each connection
and check whether the receiving command will actually use
it.

Similarly, mixing stderr into a stream can produce a
surprisingly plausible answer. This practice command uses
the intentionally absent file and deliberately makes the
diagnostic part of the count:

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 not-here.txt 2>&1 | wc -l
\end{terminal}

Here the shell connects the left command's stdout to
the pipe before applying \cmd|2>&1|. The duplicate now
points at the pipe rather than at your screen. The
counter counts whatever diagnostic line endings are
supplied and may print one instead of zero. It also
removes the actual diagnostic text from your view. It
doesn't tell you that the file was read. If you need to
keep the problem for later, save stderr to its own file
as in Chapter~\ref{ch:16}. Use a merged stream only if
the reader wants a combined human-readable report, not a
clean count.

For a chain that will be repeated, write down two things
about each stage: what data it expects, and what output
counts as success. The producer in this chapter expects
the two-line heading; the counter expects one box per
newline; the human running the commands checks both
assumptions against the source. If the source changes,
there may be no reason to change the pipe itself.
Instead you change the command that removes the heading
or choose a representation where records can be distinguished
from the heading. Keeping the intermediate for one test helps
you choose which stage needs work. It is usually faster than
trying to deduce the input from a single number at the end.
On an important job, save the output under a fresh name
after checking the stages, and decide explicitly what should
happen if the first stage fails. A message requesting the
missing file can be more useful than a clean-looking count.

\section{Use a small, deliberate test set}
An empty stream is a useful test, not just a failure case.
Use the valid donations file with a start position beyond
its end. This time the file exists; skipping past the last
line legitimately produces no box rows.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +6 donations.txt | wc -l
tail -n +6 donations.txt
printf 'Producer status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Expect a zero count, no diagnostic, and a successful
producer. Compare that with the earlier missing input.
The counter's result is identical, yet the absence of
rows has different explanations. If you'll use a zero
as an instruction to cancel a delivery or skip a pickup,
validate which explanation applies. Test both a normal
input and an empty result to see whether your workflow
mistakes missing data for a valid empty result.

Make each test fixture small enough to check by hand.
Keep the header exactly as the source format specifies.
Include one row that looks like a header and one gap you
don't want counted. Describe the result without mentioning
the pipeline: ``there are three donation boxes even though
the report is five lines.'' This check is independent of
the commands. If the count is right, run the producer again
without the counter to see if it kept the right three
rows rather than retaining a heading and omitting a box.

Decide what should become evidence for the next reader.
Keeping the total at the end with \cmd|tee| can be useful,
but the saved and displayed totals don't prove anything
independently: they are copies of the same bytes. If you
need other evidence, keep the selected rows or count the
small fixture manually. If you repeat the job for several
days, make the output pathname identify the input and
avoid overwriting yesterday's check. A pipeline lets
commands cooperate without an intermediate file; it doesn't
force you to discard data someone needs for review.

\section{Practice and cleanup}
\begin{exercise}{Skip only the first line}
Construct a pipeline that still includes the date but not
the heading. Inspect the producer separately and then count
its result. Leave the original list intact.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|tail -n +2 donations.txt| prints the date and three
box lines. Connect it to \cmd|wc -l| for four. The count
doesn't change any file; no exercise cleanup is needed.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Save at the right stage}
Count the three boxes again. Put a copy of the lines being
counted in \texttt{exercise-rows.txt}. Verify that it contains
names rather than the count.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd+tail -n +3 donations.txt | tee exercise-rows.txt | wc -l+.
Expect three on the terminal. Use \cmd|cat exercise-rows.txt|
to check the three box lines. The cleanup below includes the copy.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Don't publish the failed count}
Attempt the missing-file count. Then run the failing stage
on its own. Check each status directly and explain any
difference before treating the count as a fact.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd+tail -n +3 not-here.txt | wc -l+ then
\cmd|printf '%s\n' "$?"|. With the normal last-stage rule,
this prints zero. Run \cmd|tail -n +3 not-here.txt| alone
and check again: it fails. Neither call makes a file.
\end{solution}

After confirming \cmd|pwd| is the recorded lab directory,
remove only these known practice files with confirmation.
Omit an optional output if you didn't make it. If the directory
isn't empty, inspect the remaining entries and save anything
valuable before leaving it behind or cleaning it up.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i donations.txt box-rows.txt total.txt checked-rows.txt
rm -i exercise-rows.txt
cd ..
rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Connect output to input, and describe the bytes crossing each pipe.
\item Put \texttt{tee} where the information you want to keep passes through.
\item Check producer failures independently when a final stage could succeed with incomplete data.
\end{takeaways}

